"true"><mjx-texatom texclass=3D"ORD"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-cal mjx-i"><mjx-c=
 class=3D"mjx-c53 TEX-C"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom><mjx-mo class=3D"mj=
x-n" space=3D"4"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c2286"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-msup spac=
e=3D"4"><mjx-texatom texclass=3D"ORD"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-ds mjx-b"><mjx-c=
 class=3D"mjx-c211D TEX-A"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom><mjx-script style=
=3D"vertical-align: 0.363em;"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i" size=3D"s"><mjx-c cla=
ss=3D"mjx-c1D451 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-script></mjx-msup></mjx-math=
><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"h=
ttp://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=3D"ORD"><mi data=
-mjx-variant=3D"-tex-calligraphic" mathvariant=3D"script">S</mi></mrow><mo>=
=E2=8A=86</mo><msup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=3D"ORD"><mi mathvariant=3D"doub=
le-struck">R</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-c=
ontainer> refers to a set of allowed perturbation for the adversary; E.g. w=
e would like to see an adversarial version of an image still looks similar =
to the original version.</p>
<p>The objective is composed of an <em>inner maximization</em> problem and =
an <em>outer minimization</em> problem:</p>
<ul>
<li class=3D"has-jax"><em>Inner maximization</em>: find the most effective =
adversarial data point, <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0=
" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"182" style=3D"font-size:=
 113.1%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"tr=
ue"><mjx-texatom texclass=3D"ORD"><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-b"><mjx-c class=3D"m=
jx-c1D431 TEX-B"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom><mjx-mo class=3D"mjx-n" spa=
ce=3D"3"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c2B"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-b m=
jx-i" space=3D"3"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c1D739 TEX-BI"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx=
-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=
=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=3D"ORD"><mi=
 mathvariant=3D"bold">x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=3D"bold-italic=
">=CE=B4</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>, that leads to hig=
h loss. All the adversarial attack methods eventually come down to ways to =
maximize the loss in the inner loop.</li>
<li><em>Outer minimization</em>: find the best model parameterization such =
that the loss with the most effective attacks triggered from the inner maxi=
mization process is minimized. Naive way to train a robust model is to repl=
ace each data point with their perturbed versions, which can be multiple ad=
versarial variants of one data point.</li>
</ul>
